Note = {22nd Congress of the International Ergonomics Association-IEA, SOUTH
   KOREA, AUG 25-29, 2024},
Abstract = {Driving automation is expected to improve working conditions, support
   the present transport workforce to remain on the job longer, safely and
   healthy, and make the job more ``attractive{''} for the next generation
   of professional drivers. Yet, is this just a matter of getting
   technology into vehicles? To pluralize a predominant technocentric
   outlook on automated driving, our research adopted the work activity's
   point of view, concretely the bus driving activity, to explore how work
   experience in urban passenger transport can contribute to the design of
   future automated buses. The study followed a qualitative approach with a
   group of bus drivers providing public passenger transport service in a
   metropolitan area of Portugal. The bus drivers presented a set of
   conditions that should be considered when discussing future transport
   systems with automated buses: employment and working conditions, the
   work organization (bus services timetables) and even the urban
   environment (road infrastructure). Our findings also reveal the
   integrative nature of work experience since, in the face of a
   technological change promising to reconfigure the technical conditions
   of work, it links the actual ways of working to a possible future work
   situation with automated buses.},
