   (AHFE), Las Vegas, NV, JUL 26-30, 2015},
Abstract = {Only some years ago, the term ``Geek{''} was used to describe misfits
   that live in their own world surrounded by technology and science.
   Today, due to the omnipresence of technology and also because of famous
   TV shows, a sense of social acceptance emerged, translating the formerly
   disparaging denotation into something cool and desirable. While the term
   is nowadays colloquially accepted, research still lacks a solid
   explanation why technologies reach a certain state of desirability
   within selected user groups. This paper presents research in progress.
   The concept of a ``Geek Factor{''} as a methodological approach to match
   technologies with human personality is introduced. Harmonizing
   classified technologies with the user's personal preferences allows to
   increase the chance of desirability and attention for individualized
   services, e.g., at the point of sale in the retail business. As part of
   the complex endeavor a preliminary scoring system is outlined to measure
   the Geek Factor on a personality as well as on a technology level. As
   work in progress this paper intends to provide insights into a new
   approach for creating positive customer experiences and inspire
   researchers and practitioners for investigating the potential of geeks
   acting as brand ambassadors for technology-enabled services and
   products. Finally, starting points for future research are outlined. (C)
   2015 The Authors. Published by Elsevier B.V.},
